    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Opponent cast Thoughtseize, which is a noncreature spell on the stack. Gem3F Johnny had Stubborn Denial in hand and the mana/ability to cast it (Steam Vents can produce U, though it's tapped — but Wooded Foothills is untapped and with Leyline of the Guildpact all lands are every basic type, so fetching could produce U). Actually, looking more carefully: Steam Vents, Mountain, and Indatha Triome are all tapped. Wooded Foothills is untapped but needs to be sacrificed to fetch. With Leyline of the Guildpact, Wooded Foothills is every basic land type and can tap for any color including U. So they could tap Wooded Foothills for U and cast Stubborn Denial to counter Thoughtseize. Even without Ferocious, Stubborn Denial would counter unless opponent pays {1}, and opponent has no untapped lands (Swamp, Ifnir Deadlands x2, Urborg are their lands — need to check). Looking at opponent's board: Swamp, Ifnir Deadlands, Construct Token, Ifnir Deadlands, Nethergoyf, Urborg. The opponent likely tapped lands to cast Thoughtseize (costs {B}, so one land tapped). With Urborg making all lands Swamps, opponent could have multiple untapped lands to pay {1}. However, Stubborn Denial without Ferocious is still worth trying — it forces opponent to have and spend an extra mana. But more importantly, Gem3F Johnny declined to counter and let Thoughtseize resolve, revealing their hand. With only 3 cards in hand (Stubborn Denial, Territorial Kavu, Leyline of the Guildpact), losing any of these is significant. Actually wait — with Leyline of the Guildpact making all nonland permanents all colors, and Territorial Kavu being a creature, Kavu's power equals 5 (all basic land types). That means Ferocious would be active if Kavu were on the battlefield, but it's in hand. No Ferocious. Without Ferocious, opponent can pay {1} to avoid the counter. Opponent likely has untapped lands to pay {1}. So Stubborn Denial might not actually counter it. Still, making them pay {1} has some value, and there's a chance they're tapped out. Let me reconsider — Thoughtseize costs {B}. Opponent has Swamp, 2x Ifnir Deadlands, Urborg. With Urborg, all lands tap for B. So they tapped one land for Thoughtseize and likely have 2-3 untapped lands. They can easily pay {1}. So declining Stubborn Denial is actually reasonable since it wouldn't counter the spell. But wait — even using Stubborn Denial means it goes to graveyard and isn't revealed/lost to Thoughtseize. If they use it now (even if countered by paying {1}), they protect Territorial Kavu from being taken by Thoughtseize since the opponent would only see Kavu and Leyline. Actually no, if Stubborn Denial resolves (fails to counter), it goes to graveyard, and then Thoughtseize resolves revealing only Kavu and Leyline. That's actually a consideration — using Stubborn Denial to 'protect' the more important card. But opponent can still take Kavu. Hmm, this is more nuanced than initially apparent.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to cast Stubborn Denial in response to Thoughtseize, allowing it to resolve and reveal full hand.",
      "better_line": "Cast Stubborn Denial targeting Thoughtseize. Even though opponent likely has mana to pay {1} (no Ferocious), casting it removes Stubborn Denial from hand before Thoughtseize resolves, meaning opponent only sees Territorial Kavu and Leyline of the Guildpact — potentially protecting a more valuable card from being discarded. Additionally, Wooded Foothills with Leyline could tap for U, and sacrificing it would enable Revolt for future plays while the Stubborn Denial going to graveyard adds an instant type for potential Nethergoyf/escape considerations.",
      "type": "blunder",
