    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Casting Ragavan before combat is generally suboptimal when you have no other creatures to attack with and no tricks that benefit from pre-combat information. However, in this case Gem3F Johnny has no other creatures on board, so Ragavan won't attack this turn regardless (it doesn't have haste unless cast with Dash or given haste by Arena of Glory). That said, Arena of Glory could give haste if used with its exert ability - paying {R}, {T}, exert to add {R}{R}, and if that mana is spent on a creature spell it gains haste. Gem3F Johnny could have cast Ragavan using Arena of Glory's exert ability to give it haste, allowing it to attack this turn and potentially connect for a Treasure token and card exile. Instead, they appear to be casting it with regular mana, missing the haste opportunity. Actually, looking more carefully, the choice is just 'Ragavan' - we don't know which land they tapped. But casting Ragavan in precombat main is correct if they want to attack with it - they'd need haste though. The key question is whether they used Arena of Glory's exert to give haste. Since we can't confirm, and this is a reasonable play regardless (deploying a threat), this is only questionable.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Ragavan, Nimble Pilferer in precombat main phase",
      "better_line": "Cast Ragavan using Arena of Glory's exert ability ({R}, {T}, exert: Add {R}{R}, creature gains haste) to give Ragavan haste and attack immediately, potentially generating a Treasure token and exiling a card from opponent's library. At 6 life facing a Nethergoyf, every point of damage and resource matters.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 161,
      "player": "Gem3F Johnny",
      "decision_index": 321,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "At 6 life facing a Nethergoyf and two Eldrazi Spawn tokens, Gem3F Johnny chose to hard-cast Ragavan for {R} instead of using Dash for {1}{R}. Dash gives haste, allowing Ragavan to attack immediately and potentially generate a Treasure token and card advantage. Hard-casting means Ragavan won't attack until next turn, and at 6 life against a board that dealt 6 damage last attack, surviving to next turn is not guaranteed. With Dash, if Ragavan is removed, it would return to hand instead of dying. However, hard-casting does leave Ragavan as a permanent blocker, which could be relevant at 6 life - Ragavan could chump-block the Nethergoyf next turn to survive. The trade-off between having a blocker (hard cast) vs. getting immediate attack value and protection from removal (dash) is debatable, but at 6 life with the opponent having a large Nethergoyf, the blocking argument has merit. Still, Dash would give haste for immediate combat damage, potential Treasure + exile, and if opponent has removal Ragavan returns to hand safely.",
      "action_taken": "Hard-cast Ragavan for {R} without Dash",
      "better_line": "Cast Ragavan with Dash for {1}{R} to get haste, attack immediately for potential Treasure token and card advantage, and protect Ragavan from sorcery-speed removal by having it return to hand at end of turn",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 162,
      "player": "Gem3F Johnny",
      "decision_index": 325,
    },
